<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Colony of Dominica</h1><p class="st-meta">1763 CE – 1978 CE · capital Roseau</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Mountainous British colony between Guadeloupe and Martinique, home of the last Kalinago community in the eastern Caribbean, independent since 1978.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Dominica's rugged terrain had allowed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinago">Kalinago</a> to hold it long after their neighbours fell, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> agreed in 1748 to leave it neutral. French woodcutters and planters nevertheless settled there. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1763">Treaty of Paris</a> in 1763 gave it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, though France occupied it again from 1778 to 1783.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">Coffee</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a> and limes were grown on estates worked by enslaved Africans, while maroon bands held out in the interior until the campaigns of 1813 to 1814. In 1838 Dominica's assembly became the first in the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> with a majority of men of colour. After the assembly was weakened, the island became a crown colony in 1898, and in 1903 a reserve was set aside for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinago">Kalinago</a>. The French-based <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antillean-creole">Kwéyòl</a> remained the people's everyday language.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leeward-islands">Leeward Islands</a> until 1940 and then of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-windward-islands">British Windward Islands</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commonwealth-of-dominica">Dominica</a> joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/west-indies-federation">West Indies Federation</a>, became an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies-associated-states">Associated State</a> in 1967 and gained independence as the Commonwealth of Dominica on 3 November 1978.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/colony-of-dominica.jpg" alt="Colony of Dominica" width="320" height="164"><figcaption>Image: Agostino Brunias · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agostino_Brunias_-_View_on_the_River_Roseau,_Dominica_-_1953.14_-_Art_Institute_of_Chicago.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1763 CE – 1978 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Roseau</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English and Dominican Kwéyòl</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/dominica">Dominica</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>British Dominica</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1870&amp;focus=colony-of-dominica" aria-label="Colony of Dominica on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3004 1609 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Colony of Dominica lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3324 1865L4997 651M3324 1865L3320 1799M3324 1865L3365 1946M3324 1865L3324 1865"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4997 651h0M3320 1799h0M3365 1946h0M3324 1865h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3324 1865h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3324 1865h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Roseau. <a href="/atlas?year=1870&amp;focus=colony-of-dominica">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
